1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,680

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,000
OK, let's get started.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
As a recap of some of the stuff
that we've talked about so far,

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,440
we've formulated this
reinforcement learning problem

5
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:16,840
where we have
states and actions.

6
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:20,600
We have some trajectories
that we might be collecting,

7
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:22,777
which are sequences
of states and actions.

8
00:00:22,777 --> 00:00:24,360
We have a reward
function, which tells

9
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:29,240
us how good is a particular
state and action,

10
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:31,080
and also a policy,
which is usually

11
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,960
what we're trying to learn.

12
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,800
And this is representing
the behavior

13
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,000
that we're trying to learn.

14
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,060
And then the goal of
reinforcement learning,

15
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:42,420
which we talked about
in the first lecture,

16
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:46,700
is to try to maximize the
expected sum of rewards.

17
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:53,880
So we want to produce behavior
that leads to positive outcomes.

18
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:57,200
And we can actually
mathematically write this

19
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,480
down as an expectation over
the probability distribution

20
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:03,600
over trajectories, where this
probability distribution is

21
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,480
a function of the
policy which is acting

22
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:09,560
in the world, the
environment dynamics which

23
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:13,770
might be stochastic, and of
course, the reward function.

24
00:01:13,770 --> 00:01:17,240

25
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,000
Last week on Friday, we
started talking about one way

26
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,420
to do this, which is if you
have expert demonstrations,

27
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:24,840
you could try to
imitate the expert

28
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:29,920
by trying to maximize the
likelihood of the actions

29
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,600
that the demonstrator did--
so trying to actually produce

30
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,520
a policy that leads to
actions that the expert would

31
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:37,380
have taken.

32
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,680
This is a very simple
and scalable approach,

33
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:43,000
but it cannot outperform the
demonstrator and doesn't allow

34
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,720
improvement through
trial and error.

35
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:50,080
We also introduced the notion
of an offline algorithm that

36
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:53,280
only uses an existing
data set, and no new data

37
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:56,120
from a learned policy, and
an online algorithm that

38
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:59,230
uses new data from
the learned policy.

39
00:01:59,230 --> 00:02:01,750
And today we'll be
talking about ways

40
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:03,205
in which we can
use reinforcement

41
00:02:03,205 --> 00:02:04,830
learning to outperform
the demonstrator

42
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:06,292
to improve from practice.

43
00:02:06,292 --> 00:02:07,750
And specifically,
we'll be starting

44
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:10,310
to talk about online
reinforcement learning

45
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:13,150
algorithms.

46
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:15,110
Particularly, our first
online RL algorithm

47
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:17,070
will be policy gradients.

48
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:19,470
We'll talk about a couple of
different variants of policy

49
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:23,570
gradients and introduce both the
mathematical derivation for it,

50
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:26,150
as well as some of the
intuition behind it.

51
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:28,350
And policy gradients
are the basis

52
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:31,310
for some popular reinforcement
learning algorithms, including

53
00:02:31,310 --> 00:02:35,270
ones that are used to train
legged robots, algorithms that

54
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:37,870
are used to train
language models,

55
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:41,590
and are also part of
the default project.

56
00:02:41,590 --> 00:02:43,270
We won't get the
complete algorithm

57
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:47,990
that was used to train these
today, but we'll get most of--

58
00:02:47,990 --> 00:02:49,650
or halfway there, basically.

59
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:54,270
And by Friday, we'll have
most of the algorithm for it.

60
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:55,830
And then the key
goals will be to try

61
00:02:55,830 --> 00:02:57,930
to get the intuition
behind policy gradients,

62
00:02:57,930 --> 00:03:00,570
how to implement them,
and when to use them.

63
00:03:00,570 --> 00:03:03,510

64
00:03:03,510 --> 00:03:04,430
OK.

65
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:08,510
So the outline of an online
reinforcement learning algorithm

66
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:11,010
is going to look
something like this.

67
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:15,690
So first, we need to initialize
our policy in some way.

68
00:03:15,690 --> 00:03:17,190
When you train a
neural network, you

69
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:18,950
need to initialize its weights.

70
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:21,883
We could initialize the
weights of our policy randomly.

71
00:03:21,883 --> 00:03:24,050
We could also initialize
it with imitation learning.

72
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:26,630
We could also use heuristics.

73
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:29,270
It depends on the
problem setting.

74
00:03:29,270 --> 00:03:31,550
And after we
initialize our policy,

75
00:03:31,550 --> 00:03:34,030
we'll run our policy
to collect some data.

76
00:03:34,030 --> 00:03:38,190
So actually, try out
different things in the world.

77
00:03:38,190 --> 00:03:42,670
Then we'll use that batch of
data to improve the policy.

78
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:44,250
And then we will
repeat that process.

79
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:47,270
We'll then collect new data
with our updated policy

80
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:51,710
and update our policy again
using that latest batch of data.

81
00:03:51,710 --> 00:03:54,650
So we're going to attempt the
task and try to get better.

82
00:03:54,650 --> 00:03:56,860
So what this might look
like is the first iteration,

83
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:59,980
we attempt whatever--

84
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:02,980
some kind of simple
2D navigation task.

85
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:04,960
The reward for some
of them is bad.

86
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:08,060
The reward for other
trajectories is OK.

87
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:10,902
And then after more
iterations of this,

88
00:04:10,902 --> 00:04:12,860
we might have a policy
that's a bit better that

89
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:15,417
is able to get a higher
reward but isn't perfect.

90
00:04:15,417 --> 00:04:16,959
So sometimes it gets
a higher reward,

91
00:04:16,959 --> 00:04:20,260
sometimes it still
gets a low reward.

92
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,500
Then we'll use that to
improve the policy again.

93
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,180
And after more
iterations, hopefully, we

94
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:30,025
will get a policy that
is able to succeed.

95
00:04:30,025 --> 00:04:32,780
So this is this notion of
online reinforcement learning

96
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:35,260
where we're collecting
data online and improving

97
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:38,318
using that data.

98
00:04:38,318 --> 00:04:39,860
So we talked about
this reinforcement

99
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,220
learning objective.

100
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:45,020
And say you have
some policy, and you

101
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:48,515
want to actually understand
how good that policy is,

102
00:04:48,515 --> 00:04:50,140
to do that, we can't
just actually look

103
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:51,682
at the weights of
the neural network.

104
00:04:51,682 --> 00:04:53,380
We actually have
to run the policy

105
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:55,380
to understand how good it is.

106
00:04:55,380 --> 00:05:01,180
And so what we'll do is to
estimate this expectation, which

107
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:03,200
is a function of the policy.

108
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,780
We can actually just run
the policy multiple times,

109
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:11,580
observe the resulting reward,
and then compute the average

110
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:13,060
over the observed reward.

111
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:16,440
And that's an estimate of
how good our policy is.

112
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:19,780

113
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:24,140
So we can then sum over the
samples from our policy.

114
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:26,912
Now, let's start to think about
how we actually-- so we have

115
00:05:26,912 --> 00:05:28,120
this trial and error process.

116
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:31,860
We collect some data and
we improve our policy.

117
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:33,680
Collecting the data
is straightforward.

118
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,740
We've talked about
how to run a policy

119
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:37,680
to get a rollout
in the environment.

120
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,320
We can sample an action,
observe the next state,

121
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:43,540
then run that through our policy
again to get a new action,

122
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:45,300
and repeat that process.

123
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:48,220
But how do we actually
then improve our policy

124
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:51,220
after we've collected that data?

125
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:56,050
So we have our objective, and
this can be written down--

126
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:58,010
we have some policy pi-theta.

127
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:04,370
And our goal is to maximize
the expected rewards when

128
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:06,110
we sample from that policy.

129
00:06:06,110 --> 00:06:09,370
So we can consider the
trajectories that are sampled

130
00:06:09,370 --> 00:06:14,250
from the policy as being drawn
by some distribution that

131
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:16,890
I'll write as p-theta of tau--

132
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:19,490
theta because the
distribution over trajectories

133
00:06:19,490 --> 00:06:24,290
is a function of the
policy that you're using.

134
00:06:24,290 --> 00:06:28,810
And our goal is to maximize
the sum of rewards.

135
00:06:28,810 --> 00:06:30,650
I'm going to use shorthand here.

136
00:06:30,650 --> 00:06:36,890
And I'm going to use shorthand
for r of the trajectory, where

137
00:06:36,890 --> 00:06:43,530
this just means the sum of the
rewards along that trajectory.

138
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:47,290
So our goal is to
maximize the rewards.

139
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:49,210
We're going to maximize
this with respect

140
00:06:49,210 --> 00:06:53,490
to our policy parameters theta.

141
00:06:53,490 --> 00:06:57,150
Now, how do we
actually optimize this?

142
00:06:57,150 --> 00:06:59,450
So we want to use gradient
descent because we typically

143
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:01,283
train neural networks
with gradient descent.

144
00:07:01,283 --> 00:07:03,810
We need to figure out the
gradient of this objective

145
00:07:03,810 --> 00:07:06,610
with regard to theta.

146
00:07:06,610 --> 00:07:15,970
And we'll refer to this
term right here as j-theta

147
00:07:15,970 --> 00:07:17,470
to be able to write it.

148
00:07:17,470 --> 00:07:20,675
This is the objective that
we're trying to maximize.

149
00:07:20,675 --> 00:07:22,050
And it's a little
bit challenging

150
00:07:22,050 --> 00:07:25,410
to try to get a
gradient of this thing

151
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:28,170
directly because
the relationship

152
00:07:28,170 --> 00:07:30,650
that this has on theta is
through the trajectories

153
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:32,210
that we sampled.

154
00:07:32,210 --> 00:07:36,850
So we can't just differentiate
through the sampling process.

155
00:07:36,850 --> 00:07:38,650
And that sampling
process also depends

156
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:42,945
on the dynamics of the world,
which we also don't know.

157
00:07:42,945 --> 00:07:44,570
So if we're going to
think about trying

158
00:07:44,570 --> 00:07:53,040
to get a gradient
of j-theta, it's

159
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:59,560
helpful to write down this
expectation as an integral.

160
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:03,880
So this expectation
can be written

161
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:14,520
as an integral of p-theta
of tau times r of tau d tau.

162
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:20,440
So this is just from the
definition of an expectation.

163
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:23,785
Now, because an
integral is a summation,

164
00:08:23,785 --> 00:08:25,160
and that's a linear
operation, we

165
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:30,200
can bring the gradient
inside the integral.

166
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:32,919
And so in particular,
what that looks

167
00:08:32,919 --> 00:08:42,600
like is the integral of
the gradient of p-theta

168
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:47,360
of tau r of tau.

169
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:50,620
Now, this doesn't really
help us directly in any way,

170
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:53,360
because we don't want to have to
evaluate this integral in order

171
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:55,200
to take a gradient.

172
00:08:55,200 --> 00:09:01,120
And at this point, there is kind
of a trick, a convenient trick

173
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:02,640
that we can use
to try to put this

174
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:04,110
into a form that's more useful.

175
00:09:04,110 --> 00:09:07,240

176
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:11,800
And in particular,
the trick is that--

177
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:14,760
we'll write it over here--

178
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:21,680
is that if you
think about p-theta

179
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:32,400
of tau times the gradient of
p-theta of tau-- actually,

180
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:34,590
sorry, times the
gradient of the log.

181
00:09:34,590 --> 00:09:41,480

182
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:44,680
Does anyone remember what
the gradient of a log is

183
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:46,060
or the derivative of log?

184
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:48,070
[INAUDIBLE]

185
00:09:48,070 --> 00:09:50,910
1 over, right?

186
00:09:50,910 --> 00:09:53,350
Then what this is equal to?

187
00:09:53,350 --> 00:10:03,367
This is equal to p-theta of tau
times 1 over p-theta of tau.

188
00:10:03,367 --> 00:10:04,950
And then we need to
use the chain rule

189
00:10:04,950 --> 00:10:06,695
to apply the gradient to this.

190
00:10:06,695 --> 00:10:08,070
And so with the
chain rule, we'll

191
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:13,510
get the gradient
of p-theta of tau.

192
00:10:13,510 --> 00:10:15,910
And these two will
then cancel out.

193
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:22,390
And this is equal to the
gradient of p-theta of tau.

194
00:10:22,390 --> 00:10:26,750
And the reason why this is a
convenient equality between

195
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:28,090
gradient of p-theta--

196
00:10:28,090 --> 00:10:29,750
and this is we
could actually then

197
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:34,990
replace the gradient of p-theta
down here with this first thing

198
00:10:34,990 --> 00:10:36,590
right here.

199
00:10:36,590 --> 00:10:41,590
If we replace this gradient
of p-theta of tau right here,

200
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:49,630
we replace this, we now get an
integral of p-theta of tau times

201
00:10:49,630 --> 00:11:03,430
grad log p-theta of tau
times r of tau d tau.

202
00:11:03,430 --> 00:11:05,630
Now, this is something
that's useful

203
00:11:05,630 --> 00:11:07,030
because we can
now represent this

204
00:11:07,030 --> 00:11:10,550
as an expectation, this
first term right here.

205
00:11:10,550 --> 00:11:14,230
And so this is equal
to an expectation

206
00:11:14,230 --> 00:11:22,442
of tau sampled from
p-theta of tau of grad log

207
00:11:22,442 --> 00:11:27,810
p-theta of tau times r of tau.

208
00:11:27,810 --> 00:11:30,710

209
00:11:30,710 --> 00:11:34,310
So what we did there is
we used this log trick

210
00:11:34,310 --> 00:11:38,590
to then get the p-theta out here
and then get an expectation.

211
00:11:38,590 --> 00:11:41,830
And we're starting to get to
something that's actually more

212
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:43,430
what we can evaluate.

213
00:11:43,430 --> 00:11:45,620
And in particular,
this expectation

214
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:47,980
is something that we can
estimate with samples.

215
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:52,540
And now theta is actually
appearing on the inside here.

216
00:11:52,540 --> 00:11:55,460
And that means that
this is actually

217
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:57,700
getting closer to something
that we can evaluate.

218
00:11:57,700 --> 00:12:00,160
We still have this
term right here,

219
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:03,380
which doesn't look
like pi of a given S.

220
00:12:03,380 --> 00:12:05,388
So we need to work on this term.

221
00:12:05,388 --> 00:12:07,180
But we're starting to
get towards something

222
00:12:07,180 --> 00:12:09,300
that we can evaluate.

223
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:12,480
So the question is, how is this
more useful than the first term,

224
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:14,220
because we still
have the sampling,

225
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:17,220
and the issue with
the first case

226
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:20,200
was that we can't differentiate
through the sampling?

227
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:21,700
Now, the thing
that's different here

228
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:24,740
is that theta actually appears
inside the expectation.

229
00:12:24,740 --> 00:12:26,700
Now we're not going to
be able to differentiate

230
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:29,420
through the sampling, still.

231
00:12:29,420 --> 00:12:31,420
But the thing that's
useful here is

232
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:34,420
that we will actually
be able to differentiate

233
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:36,220
with respect to theta.

234
00:12:36,220 --> 00:12:39,380
And we're still not going to
differentiate in this part,

235
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:42,240
but we'll be able to get an
estimate that's still useful.

236
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:45,620
And we could potentially
draw many, many samples.

237
00:12:45,620 --> 00:12:47,740
And then measure this
quantity to get actually

238
00:12:47,740 --> 00:12:52,567
a pretty accurate representation
of the gradient in a way that

239
00:12:52,567 --> 00:12:53,650
actually depends on theta.

240
00:12:53,650 --> 00:12:57,640

241
00:12:57,640 --> 00:12:58,140
OK.

242
00:12:58,140 --> 00:13:00,970
Let's talk a little bit
about this term right here.

243
00:13:00,970 --> 00:13:03,566

244
00:13:03,566 --> 00:13:06,420
And actually, maybe in
case it's useful in terms

245
00:13:06,420 --> 00:13:09,960
of where we're at so far, we can
show that on the slides as well.

246
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:16,820
So we looked at this j of theta,
wrote it down as an integral.

247
00:13:16,820 --> 00:13:19,900
We found this convenient
identity over here,

248
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:23,700
where p grad log p
is equal to grad p.

249
00:13:23,700 --> 00:13:27,540
And then we can use that to
derive this second equation

250
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:30,660
right here.

251
00:13:30,660 --> 00:13:33,340
And now we're right here.

252
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:37,860
So for this part,
for grad log p,

253
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:40,060
this is something
that we can write out.

254
00:13:40,060 --> 00:13:41,780
And if we use--

255
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:51,570
if we remember that p-theta
of tau equals p of S1 times pi

256
00:13:51,570 --> 00:13:59,490
of at given St and P of
St plus 1, given St at,

257
00:13:59,490 --> 00:14:06,090
and then with a product over t.

258
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:10,290
Then when we take the log
of this, it's over there,

259
00:14:10,290 --> 00:14:19,170
the log of p-theta of
tau is equal to the log

260
00:14:19,170 --> 00:14:35,090
of p of S1 plus the sum over T
of the log of pi plus the log

261
00:14:35,090 --> 00:14:37,020
of the dynamics.

262
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:41,930

263
00:14:41,930 --> 00:14:49,890
And then once we actually take
the gradient of log p tau, then

264
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:52,290
the gradient of this
with respect to theta,

265
00:14:52,290 --> 00:14:54,870
theta doesn't affect the
initial state distribution.

266
00:14:54,870 --> 00:14:57,450
It also doesn't affect
the dynamics in any way.

267
00:14:57,450 --> 00:15:05,570
And so the gradient
of log p-theta of tau

268
00:15:05,570 --> 00:15:09,650
is equal to just the gradient
of this term right here.

269
00:15:09,650 --> 00:15:24,062
So the gradient of log
pi-theta of a given S.

270
00:15:24,062 --> 00:15:25,770
And so this is really
useful because this

271
00:15:25,770 --> 00:15:31,183
means that we can actually
plug in this over there.

272
00:15:31,183 --> 00:15:33,350
And this is actually something
that we can evaluate.

273
00:15:33,350 --> 00:15:36,050
We can evaluate
the gradient of--

274
00:15:36,050 --> 00:15:41,040
or the log-- the
gradient of log pi.

275
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:43,400
And this is just
taking a backward pass

276
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:46,870
through the log of the
output of our neural network.

277
00:15:46,870 --> 00:15:50,240

278
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:55,120
And so then we actually get a
gradient that we can evaluate.

279
00:15:55,120 --> 00:16:08,320
So the gradient that we
get when we plug things in,

280
00:16:08,320 --> 00:16:13,600
the gradient of j-theta
when we plug this in,

281
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:19,840
is we have an expectation
over trajectories

282
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:23,280
sampled from our policy.

283
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:27,440
And inside that expectation,
we can bring the gradient

284
00:16:27,440 --> 00:16:29,360
inside that summation.

285
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:35,600
And so we'll have a sum
over time steps of grad log

286
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:41,000
pi of at given St, right here.

287
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,800
And then we still
have our r of t

288
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:47,320
right here, so we can
write that as r of tau.

289
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:55,370
We could also write it as a
summation of r of St comma at.

290
00:16:55,370 --> 00:16:58,760

291
00:16:58,760 --> 00:17:02,320
So this is what's called the
policy gradient or the vanilla

292
00:17:02,320 --> 00:17:03,920
policy gradient.

293
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:07,960
And this is a lot
nicer than the version

294
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:13,079
up there because we can sample
trajectories from our policy,

295
00:17:13,079 --> 00:17:14,560
compute their reward.

296
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:19,319
Also, compute the
gradient of each of log

297
00:17:19,319 --> 00:17:21,760
of pi for each of the actions.

298
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:25,880
And then sum those
together, multiply,

299
00:17:25,880 --> 00:17:27,760
and we can get a
gradient that we

300
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:30,720
can apply to the weights
of our neural network.

301
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:32,990
That's actually leading
to a higher reward.

302
00:17:32,990 --> 00:17:36,200

303
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:41,710
So again, on the
slides, we wrote down

304
00:17:41,710 --> 00:17:44,070
what the probability
of a trajectory is.

305
00:17:44,070 --> 00:17:46,790
If you take the log
of that, you get

306
00:17:46,790 --> 00:17:49,950
a summation instead
of a product,

307
00:17:49,950 --> 00:17:53,190
and the log of each of
the individual terms.

308
00:17:53,190 --> 00:17:59,630
Then if we plug that
into our gradient

309
00:17:59,630 --> 00:18:03,110
and we take the
gradient of log p tau,

310
00:18:03,110 --> 00:18:06,230
then the first term
and the last term

311
00:18:06,230 --> 00:18:10,630
go away because they
don't depend on theta.

312
00:18:10,630 --> 00:18:14,970
And then we get this final
term when we plug it in.

313
00:18:14,970 --> 00:18:18,190

314
00:18:18,190 --> 00:18:19,530
So this is the gradient.

315
00:18:19,530 --> 00:18:21,830
When we run policy gradient,
we'll collect data.

316
00:18:21,830 --> 00:18:25,070
And then we'll
evaluate this equation.

317
00:18:25,070 --> 00:18:28,270
And then we'll use that
to represent the gradient

318
00:18:28,270 --> 00:18:30,310
of the weights of the policy.

319
00:18:30,310 --> 00:18:34,190
And then run gradient descent,
apply that to our policy,

320
00:18:34,190 --> 00:18:35,380
and then repeat the process.

321
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:41,030

322
00:18:41,030 --> 00:18:43,370
So yeah, we also talked
about this verbally.

323
00:18:43,370 --> 00:18:45,550
But in order to estimate
this gradient here,

324
00:18:45,550 --> 00:18:47,630
we'll run the policy
to collect some data.

325
00:18:47,630 --> 00:18:50,890
We'll then improve the
policy using that data.

326
00:18:50,890 --> 00:18:55,590
In particular, you can
think of the first summation

327
00:18:55,590 --> 00:18:59,410
as collecting n samples
for that expectation.

328
00:18:59,410 --> 00:19:01,895
And ideally, n is larger
because the higher n

329
00:19:01,895 --> 00:19:03,270
is, the better an
estimate you're

330
00:19:03,270 --> 00:19:07,990
going to get of the
expectation of the integral.

331
00:19:07,990 --> 00:19:10,150
And then once we
evaluate this gradient

332
00:19:10,150 --> 00:19:12,210
that we're estimating
with samples,

333
00:19:12,210 --> 00:19:15,370
we'll then take a gradient
step on our policy parameters.

334
00:19:15,370 --> 00:19:18,602

335
00:19:18,602 --> 00:19:20,310
And so the full
algorithm looks something

336
00:19:20,310 --> 00:19:23,630
like sampling trajectories
from our policy,

337
00:19:23,630 --> 00:19:30,230
evaluating an estimate of the
gradient using those samples,

338
00:19:30,230 --> 00:19:34,070
applying that to the weights,
and then repeating this process

339
00:19:34,070 --> 00:19:37,780
to collect a new batch of
data, recomputing the gradient,

340
00:19:37,780 --> 00:19:40,198
and improving the policy.

341
00:19:40,198 --> 00:19:41,740
So the question is,
what does it look

342
00:19:41,740 --> 00:19:46,170
like to use the data to
estimate this gradient term--

343
00:19:46,170 --> 00:19:49,260
the first term or
the second term?

344
00:19:49,260 --> 00:19:50,560
The first term.

345
00:19:50,560 --> 00:19:51,060
Yeah.

346
00:19:51,060 --> 00:19:56,242
So when we're using the data to
compute this first term, what

347
00:19:56,242 --> 00:19:58,700
we're doing is we're getting
a bunch of states and actions,

348
00:19:58,700 --> 00:19:59,893
a bunch of trajectories.

349
00:19:59,893 --> 00:20:02,060
We're getting n trajectories
at each-- in this case,

350
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:04,140
have length t time steps.

351
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:06,820
And we'll be taking
the states and actions

352
00:20:06,820 --> 00:20:09,540
in those trajectories,
passing the state

353
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:12,860
through the policy
as a forward pass

354
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:14,740
to get the probability
of our actions.

355
00:20:14,740 --> 00:20:19,430
We'll then evaluate the action
in the data, the probability

356
00:20:19,430 --> 00:20:23,380
of that action in the data under
the distribution, the policy

357
00:20:23,380 --> 00:20:23,920
output.

358
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:26,888
So it's like a
categorical distribution.

359
00:20:26,888 --> 00:20:28,180
We'll then get the probability.

360
00:20:28,180 --> 00:20:30,452
We'll take the log
of that probability.

361
00:20:30,452 --> 00:20:32,160
And then we take the
gradient right here,

362
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:33,720
which will just be
a forward pass--

363
00:20:33,720 --> 00:20:36,900
sorry, a backward pass
through the network

364
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:41,700
after taking the log of the
policy output for that action.

365
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:44,500
So this is called the REINFORCE
algorithm or the vanilla policy

366
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:46,672
gradient algorithm.

367
00:20:46,672 --> 00:20:47,380
So this came out.

368
00:20:47,380 --> 00:20:49,220
This is just r of tau.

369
00:20:49,220 --> 00:20:52,940
So I'm using r of tau
as shorthand for this,

370
00:20:52,940 --> 00:20:57,460
just because I didn't want to
write the summation five times.

371
00:20:57,460 --> 00:21:00,777
But yeah, this is
exactly r of tau.

372
00:21:00,777 --> 00:21:01,360
Yeah, exactly.

373
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:03,068
So this is an estimate
because we're only

374
00:21:03,068 --> 00:21:05,840
going to be taking n samples
to estimate this expectation.

375
00:21:05,840 --> 00:21:11,205
If you took infinite,
then this would be exact.

376
00:21:11,205 --> 00:21:13,330
Of course, taking infinite
samples is not possible.

377
00:21:13,330 --> 00:21:14,120
The gradient.

378
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:14,620
Yeah.

379
00:21:14,620 --> 00:21:16,328
So the gradient right
here, this is going

380
00:21:16,328 --> 00:21:18,500
to depend on the architecture.

381
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:21,700
The question was, do we
compute this by hand?

382
00:21:21,700 --> 00:21:24,200
You can use automatic
differentiation.

383
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:26,158
So whatever library,
PyTorch or whatever,

384
00:21:26,158 --> 00:21:27,700
that can compute
that for you, so you

385
00:21:27,700 --> 00:21:30,220
don't have to write it out.

386
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:30,720
Great.

387
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:32,970
So let's talk about some of
the intuition behind this.

388
00:21:32,970 --> 00:21:34,330
So there's the math.

389
00:21:34,330 --> 00:21:37,530
But what does this
gradient actually mean?

390
00:21:37,530 --> 00:21:39,970
So if you look at
this, maybe let's

391
00:21:39,970 --> 00:21:42,170
start by looking
at the first term.

392
00:21:42,170 --> 00:21:48,170
The first term if we think
back to imitation learning,

393
00:21:48,170 --> 00:21:50,770
this first term is actually
the same as the gradient

394
00:21:50,770 --> 00:21:52,270
of the imitation
learning objective.

395
00:21:52,270 --> 00:21:53,770
So in imitation
learning, we were

396
00:21:53,770 --> 00:21:57,730
trying to mimic actions from
some demonstration data set

397
00:21:57,730 --> 00:22:00,570
by maximizing the
log probability

398
00:22:00,570 --> 00:22:03,730
or minimizing the
negative log probability.

399
00:22:03,730 --> 00:22:05,390
And if you take the
gradient of that,

400
00:22:05,390 --> 00:22:10,568
it's similarly going to be the
gradient of the log probability.

401
00:22:10,568 --> 00:22:12,610
So you can think about
this first term as saying,

402
00:22:12,610 --> 00:22:15,810
I'm going to basically
try to increase

403
00:22:15,810 --> 00:22:21,290
the likelihood of the
actions in the online data.

404
00:22:21,290 --> 00:22:23,370
But then for the
second term, it's

405
00:22:23,370 --> 00:22:27,150
weighting that by the
reward of that trajectory.

406
00:22:27,150 --> 00:22:30,210
How good was that trajectory?

407
00:22:30,210 --> 00:22:32,870
And so you can think about
this as the imitation gradient,

408
00:22:32,870 --> 00:22:38,010
but weighted by the sum of
rewards seen in the data.

409
00:22:38,010 --> 00:22:39,970
And so essentially,
you're going to increase

410
00:22:39,970 --> 00:22:43,090
the likelihood of actions
that had high reward

411
00:22:43,090 --> 00:22:46,250
and decrease the
likelihood of actions

412
00:22:46,250 --> 00:22:49,953
that were taken in negative
reward trajectories.

413
00:22:49,953 --> 00:22:51,370
And so you're
actually going to be

414
00:22:51,370 --> 00:22:53,490
imitating your own
data, the data that

415
00:22:53,490 --> 00:22:56,330
was collected by the policy.

416
00:22:56,330 --> 00:23:02,626
But depending on whether it's
high reward or low reward,

417
00:23:02,626 --> 00:23:05,343
you actually might have
a negative gradient.

418
00:23:05,343 --> 00:23:07,010
If it's negative
reward, that's actually

419
00:23:07,010 --> 00:23:08,770
going to push you away
from the bad stuff

420
00:23:08,770 --> 00:23:10,760
and push you towards
the good stuff.

421
00:23:10,760 --> 00:23:11,970
[INAUDIBLE]

422
00:23:11,970 --> 00:23:12,470
Yeah.

423
00:23:12,470 --> 00:23:14,510
So this is inside
the first summation.

424
00:23:14,510 --> 00:23:16,503
You can see the eye
comes up right here.

425
00:23:16,503 --> 00:23:18,170
So we're summing a
bunch of directories,

426
00:23:18,170 --> 00:23:21,628
and then, yeah, this is all
within the summation over n.

427
00:23:21,628 --> 00:23:23,170
So the question is,
is the difference

428
00:23:23,170 --> 00:23:24,930
between this and
imitation learning where

429
00:23:24,930 --> 00:23:26,810
imitation learning you're
either right or wrong,

430
00:23:26,810 --> 00:23:28,518
or here you're like,
this amount of right

431
00:23:28,518 --> 00:23:31,360
or this amount of wrong?

432
00:23:31,360 --> 00:23:34,120
So you can apply this--

433
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:36,640
well, in principle, you
can apply this in scenarios

434
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:38,600
where the reward is very sparse.

435
00:23:38,600 --> 00:23:40,560
And so you can actually
apply it in scenarios

436
00:23:40,560 --> 00:23:44,080
where the reward is just 1 if
you succeeded, and 0 otherwise.

437
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:46,860
Now, this algorithm may not do
very well in those scenarios,

438
00:23:46,860 --> 00:23:48,517
and we'll talk about that.

439
00:23:48,517 --> 00:23:50,600
One of the key differences
with imitation learning

440
00:23:50,600 --> 00:23:53,460
is that you're actually doing
this on your own rollouts.

441
00:23:53,460 --> 00:23:55,860
Actually, the policy is
collecting its own data

442
00:23:55,860 --> 00:23:59,440
and then applying this
objective to its own data.

443
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:00,982
And so that's one
of the differences.

444
00:24:00,982 --> 00:24:02,357
And then second
is we're actually

445
00:24:02,357 --> 00:24:04,600
using this reward, whereas
in imitation learning,

446
00:24:04,600 --> 00:24:07,880
you're just blindly imitating
all the data without any notion

447
00:24:07,880 --> 00:24:09,420
of how good the data is.

448
00:24:09,420 --> 00:24:11,740
And it assumes that
it's good data.

449
00:24:11,740 --> 00:24:12,240
Yeah.

450
00:24:12,240 --> 00:24:16,940
How do you sample the first
state of the trajectory?

451
00:24:16,940 --> 00:24:18,400
This is something
that's typically

452
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:20,780
defined by the problem setting.

453
00:24:20,780 --> 00:24:23,400
So in some problem
settings, if you're

454
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:27,782
trying to train an agent to
play pinball or something,

455
00:24:27,782 --> 00:24:30,490
then the initial state will just
be given by the pinball machine.

456
00:24:30,490 --> 00:24:33,200

457
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:36,240
In other cases, in
language modeling,

458
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:39,760
the initial state or observation
might be the first question

459
00:24:39,760 --> 00:24:41,480
that the user asked.

460
00:24:41,480 --> 00:24:45,060
So it's usually thought of as
a property of the environment.

461
00:24:45,060 --> 00:24:46,560
So the question is,
can you use this

462
00:24:46,560 --> 00:24:48,920
to improve imitation
learning by weighting

463
00:24:48,920 --> 00:24:50,520
the trajectories by reward?

464
00:24:50,520 --> 00:24:52,120
Yeah, you could
definitely do that.

465
00:24:52,120 --> 00:24:56,200
We will also talk about some of
the good things and bad things

466
00:24:56,200 --> 00:24:59,285
about this objective
in the coming slides.

467
00:24:59,285 --> 00:25:00,660
So the question
was, in practice,

468
00:25:00,660 --> 00:25:02,300
does it make sense to start
with imitation learning,

469
00:25:02,300 --> 00:25:03,925
run that for some
number of iterations,

470
00:25:03,925 --> 00:25:06,160
and then switch to this?

471
00:25:06,160 --> 00:25:07,680
That can definitely make sense.

472
00:25:07,680 --> 00:25:10,760
If you start completely from
a randomly initialized policy,

473
00:25:10,760 --> 00:25:12,600
it will do random stuff.

474
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:16,320
And if you have a very dense
reward signal, that can be OK.

475
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:19,420
But in other scenarios, if you
have access to demonstrations,

476
00:25:19,420 --> 00:25:22,060
then initializing it
with demonstrations

477
00:25:22,060 --> 00:25:25,160
can be a very good
fit, and then it

478
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:30,082
will allow you to improve on
top of your initial policy.

479
00:25:30,082 --> 00:25:32,790
So the question is, if you don't
have a good conception of what's

480
00:25:32,790 --> 00:25:35,910
good and bad, what the
reward function should be,

481
00:25:35,910 --> 00:25:37,950
can you use imitation
learning to get

482
00:25:37,950 --> 00:25:39,790
an initial sense for that?

483
00:25:39,790 --> 00:25:42,990
We'll talk about reward
learning in a few lectures

484
00:25:42,990 --> 00:25:47,150
and how you can potentially
use expert data for that.

485
00:25:47,150 --> 00:25:52,190
In practice, it's challenging.

486
00:25:52,190 --> 00:25:54,950
So we talked about how
this intuitively increasing

487
00:25:54,950 --> 00:25:57,990
likelihood on high
reward trajectories

488
00:25:57,990 --> 00:26:01,430
and decreasing likelihood on
negative reward trajectories.

489
00:26:01,430 --> 00:26:04,050
If we actually look
at this visually,

490
00:26:04,050 --> 00:26:06,050
say that we took a
few trajectories--

491
00:26:06,050 --> 00:26:08,530
one of them had good reward,
one of them had bad reward--

492
00:26:08,530 --> 00:26:12,310
and this red is showing
our initial distribution

493
00:26:12,310 --> 00:26:14,790
over trajectories, what
this gradient is going to do

494
00:26:14,790 --> 00:26:17,410
is it's going to increase the
likelihood on the green part.

495
00:26:17,410 --> 00:26:19,390
So you'll get something
that looks like this.

496
00:26:19,390 --> 00:26:21,490
And then it'll decrease the
likelihood on the red part,

497
00:26:21,490 --> 00:26:23,365
so you'll get something
that looks like this.

498
00:26:23,365 --> 00:26:25,910
And it'll encourage
the policy to take

499
00:26:25,910 --> 00:26:28,500
actions that are more like
the high-reward trajectory.

500
00:26:28,500 --> 00:26:32,270
[INAUDIBLE]

501
00:26:32,270 --> 00:26:35,390
In even more brief terms,
you can think of this

502
00:26:35,390 --> 00:26:39,830
as trying to do more of the good
stuff and less of the bad stuff

503
00:26:39,830 --> 00:26:41,767
that you observe.

504
00:26:41,767 --> 00:26:43,350
And then you can
also think about this

505
00:26:43,350 --> 00:26:46,410
as a formalization
of trial and error,

506
00:26:46,410 --> 00:26:50,170
where you're actually going
to try to do different things.

507
00:26:50,170 --> 00:26:52,690
And if you see good
stuff happening,

508
00:26:52,690 --> 00:26:54,170
then you'll try to
do more of that.

509
00:26:54,170 --> 00:26:58,110
And if you make a mistake, then
you'll try to stop doing that.

510
00:26:58,110 --> 00:26:59,290
Cool.

511
00:26:59,290 --> 00:27:02,550
So now I have a
question for you.

512
00:27:02,550 --> 00:27:04,130
So let's look at an example.

513
00:27:04,130 --> 00:27:05,910
Say that we're trying
to have a humanoid

514
00:27:05,910 --> 00:27:08,790
learn how to walk in
simulation, and we'll

515
00:27:08,790 --> 00:27:11,110
define the reward function
to be the forward velocity

516
00:27:11,110 --> 00:27:12,043
of the robot.

517
00:27:12,043 --> 00:27:14,210
This means that the reward
function will be positive

518
00:27:14,210 --> 00:27:15,890
if it's moving forward.

519
00:27:15,890 --> 00:27:18,870
It may also be negative if
the robot is moving backwards.

520
00:27:18,870 --> 00:27:23,350
It might be 0 if the
robot is not moving.

521
00:27:23,350 --> 00:27:25,820
First, we'll sample
from our policy.

522
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:28,660
Say we have a policy that's
not very good at the beginning.

523
00:27:28,660 --> 00:27:31,380
So in the first trajectory,
maybe it falls backwards.

524
00:27:31,380 --> 00:27:33,620
In the second
trajectory, maybe it

525
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:36,140
falls forward, which will
actually technically give it

526
00:27:36,140 --> 00:27:37,380
positive reward.

527
00:27:37,380 --> 00:27:40,980
So the sum of rewards
will be positive.

528
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:43,460
Maybe another trajectory,
it manages to stand still.

529
00:27:43,460 --> 00:27:46,460
So the rewards
would be 0 for that.

530
00:27:46,460 --> 00:27:49,900
In another trajectory, maybe
it takes one small step forward

531
00:27:49,900 --> 00:27:51,460
and then falls backwards.

532
00:27:51,460 --> 00:27:55,420
And so the initial reward at
the beginning of the trajectory

533
00:27:55,420 --> 00:27:57,320
would actually be
getting positive rewards.

534
00:27:57,320 --> 00:27:59,653
And then later on, it would
be getting negative rewards,

535
00:27:59,653 --> 00:28:02,060
and the sum of rewards
would be negative.

536
00:28:02,060 --> 00:28:03,500
And then maybe the
last trajectory

537
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:05,580
is it takes one
large step backwards,

538
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:07,480
and then a small step forwards.

539
00:28:07,480 --> 00:28:11,260
And in this case, it would
have negative rewards, and then

540
00:28:11,260 --> 00:28:13,540
some positive rewards,
but the sum of rewards

541
00:28:13,540 --> 00:28:16,500
would still be negative.

542
00:28:16,500 --> 00:28:22,340
Now my question for you is,
what will the gradient that we

543
00:28:22,340 --> 00:28:24,900
defined here, what will
it encourage the policy

544
00:28:24,900 --> 00:28:28,380
to do if this is your data
set, if these are the five

545
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:33,440
trajectories that you sampled?

546
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:35,440
This is going to be one
of our simpler examples,

547
00:28:35,440 --> 00:28:37,500
and then we'll move to
more complex examples

548
00:28:37,500 --> 00:28:39,220
in a couple more slides.

549
00:28:39,220 --> 00:28:41,520
So I'd encourage you to
pair up for a minute,

550
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:43,220
discuss it briefly,
and then we'll

551
00:28:43,220 --> 00:28:47,020
come back and share
in 1 or 2 minutes.

552
00:28:47,020 --> 00:28:49,420
OK, let's come back.

553
00:28:49,420 --> 00:28:54,700
If you want to share your
answer, can you raise your hand?

554
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:55,200
OK.

555
00:28:55,200 --> 00:28:56,575
So the gradient
will encourage it

556
00:28:56,575 --> 00:29:00,940
to fall forwards and
potentially stand still.

557
00:29:00,940 --> 00:29:01,440
Cool.

558
00:29:01,440 --> 00:29:03,580
That's mostly correct.

559
00:29:03,580 --> 00:29:06,080
So if we think through
each of these trajectories,

560
00:29:06,080 --> 00:29:09,540
the first one, the reward
will be negative here.

561
00:29:09,540 --> 00:29:11,385
It will also be negative.

562
00:29:11,385 --> 00:29:13,260
The sum of rewards over
the entire trajectory

563
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:15,380
will be negative
for 1, 4, and 5.

564
00:29:15,380 --> 00:29:20,140
It should be about 0 for
3, and for 2, the sum

565
00:29:20,140 --> 00:29:21,250
should be positive.

566
00:29:21,250 --> 00:29:24,410
And so what this means is
you'll get negative gradients

567
00:29:24,410 --> 00:29:27,830
for any actions that were
taken in 1, 4, and 5.

568
00:29:27,830 --> 00:29:30,690
You'll get positive gradients
for all the actions taken in 2.

569
00:29:30,690 --> 00:29:34,850
And you'll get roughly 0
gradients for all the actions

570
00:29:34,850 --> 00:29:37,210
that are taken in trajectory 3.

571
00:29:37,210 --> 00:29:40,330
And so this means that it will
encourage it to fall forwards.

572
00:29:40,330 --> 00:29:43,090
And it'll encourage it
to not do the things that

573
00:29:43,090 --> 00:29:45,270
were in 1, 4, and 5.

574
00:29:45,270 --> 00:29:47,490
So it will discourage
it from taking steps

575
00:29:47,490 --> 00:29:49,770
backwards or forwards and
will also discourage it

576
00:29:49,770 --> 00:29:51,810
from falling backwards.

577
00:29:51,810 --> 00:29:52,930
Cool.

578
00:29:52,930 --> 00:29:53,470
So yeah.

579
00:29:53,470 --> 00:29:55,890
So we encourage the policy
to fall forwards and not

580
00:29:55,890 --> 00:29:57,050
take steps.

581
00:29:57,050 --> 00:30:00,243
This is distressing because
we don't want our policy

582
00:30:00,243 --> 00:30:00,910
to fall forward.

583
00:30:00,910 --> 00:30:03,530
We want to take steps.

584
00:30:03,530 --> 00:30:05,050
And so this is
kind of an example

585
00:30:05,050 --> 00:30:09,430
of how the gradient that we're
getting is noisy, high variance.

586
00:30:09,430 --> 00:30:12,810
It means that depending on
what these trajectories are,

587
00:30:12,810 --> 00:30:14,728
the gradient might
vary drastically.

588
00:30:14,728 --> 00:30:16,770
So if you do actually have
some trajectories that

589
00:30:16,770 --> 00:30:19,250
are walking forwards,
you might actually

590
00:30:19,250 --> 00:30:21,250
get a very different
gradient estimate

591
00:30:21,250 --> 00:30:23,230
from this set of trajectories.

592
00:30:23,230 --> 00:30:25,570
And so that's the definition
of having noise or having

593
00:30:25,570 --> 00:30:27,430
high variance.

594
00:30:27,430 --> 00:30:29,930
So the question is, why are we
getting multiple trajectories

595
00:30:29,930 --> 00:30:30,830
from the same policy?

596
00:30:30,830 --> 00:30:32,942
Why not just get one?

597
00:30:32,942 --> 00:30:34,150
There's two reasons for this.

598
00:30:34,150 --> 00:30:36,750
One is that the policy
is not deterministic.

599
00:30:36,750 --> 00:30:38,922
And so if we want to explore
different strategies,

600
00:30:38,922 --> 00:30:41,130
we will get different samples
of different strategies

601
00:30:41,130 --> 00:30:42,590
because it's non-deterministic.

602
00:30:42,590 --> 00:30:44,090
And the second reason
is also the environment

603
00:30:44,090 --> 00:30:45,030
might be stochastic.

604
00:30:45,030 --> 00:30:47,410
And so even if you have
a deterministic policy,

605
00:30:47,410 --> 00:30:48,990
you might see
different outcomes,

606
00:30:48,990 --> 00:30:53,970
and this will help you learn
about those different outcomes.

607
00:30:53,970 --> 00:30:58,130
There's one thing that we can do
about this that we can already

608
00:30:58,130 --> 00:31:01,810
directly improve the gradient
in a fairly simple way.

609
00:31:01,810 --> 00:31:04,850
And in particular, one
observation that we might make

610
00:31:04,850 --> 00:31:07,490
is that if you're in the
middle of a trajectory

611
00:31:07,490 --> 00:31:10,930
and you take an
action, that action

612
00:31:10,930 --> 00:31:14,650
has no influence
on previous states.

613
00:31:14,650 --> 00:31:17,970
And right now, the gradient
that is using the rewards at all

614
00:31:17,970 --> 00:31:21,130
states in the trajectory--

615
00:31:21,130 --> 00:31:22,930
in the gradient
update, and it's even

616
00:31:22,930 --> 00:31:26,490
telling us to take actions
later in the trajectory

617
00:31:26,490 --> 00:31:31,050
if the reward earlier in the
trajectory was good or bad.

618
00:31:31,050 --> 00:31:33,490
And so one of the
things that we can do

619
00:31:33,490 --> 00:31:35,530
is if one of our
trajectories was

620
00:31:35,530 --> 00:31:38,530
to take a large step backwards
and then a small step forwards,

621
00:31:38,530 --> 00:31:41,130
then we should actually be able
to learn from this trajectory

622
00:31:41,130 --> 00:31:44,850
that taking a small
step forward is good.

623
00:31:44,850 --> 00:31:51,450
And the way that we can do
that is instead of having this

624
00:31:51,450 --> 00:31:58,770
be a sum from t equals 1 to
T, what we can instead do

625
00:31:58,770 --> 00:32:03,730
is we can actually have
this summation start at t.

626
00:32:03,730 --> 00:32:07,930
And so we can have
this essentially be--

627
00:32:07,930 --> 00:32:13,790
let's use a different letter.

628
00:32:13,790 --> 00:32:17,800
So we'll have t prime
that is starting at t

629
00:32:17,800 --> 00:32:23,680
and going to capital T. And
now this is reward of St prime,

630
00:32:23,680 --> 00:32:25,000
at prime.

631
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:30,720
And by having it only
look at future rewards,

632
00:32:30,720 --> 00:32:32,960
this means that we're now
incorporating the fact

633
00:32:32,960 --> 00:32:36,740
that current actions can
only affect the future,

634
00:32:36,740 --> 00:32:37,770
and not affect the past.

635
00:32:37,770 --> 00:32:38,920
[INAUDIBLE]

636
00:32:38,920 --> 00:32:39,500
Oh, yes.

637
00:32:39,500 --> 00:32:39,720
Sorry.

638
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:41,470
One other thing that
you have to do here--

639
00:32:41,470 --> 00:32:45,800
yes, good point-- is to
actually move this summation

640
00:32:45,800 --> 00:32:48,420
inside this summation here.

641
00:32:48,420 --> 00:32:48,940
[INAUDIBLE]

642
00:32:48,940 --> 00:32:49,440
Yeah.

643
00:32:49,440 --> 00:32:50,800
Why can we do this?

644
00:32:50,800 --> 00:32:56,240
So the reason is that we
know that our action at--

645
00:32:56,240 --> 00:33:00,580
let's say our action
at time equals 100.

646
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:07,680
So let's say if t equals 100,
so we're looking at a100.

647
00:33:07,680 --> 00:33:13,280
We know that this 100 is only
going to influence rewards

648
00:33:13,280 --> 00:33:14,480
in the future.

649
00:33:14,480 --> 00:33:19,900
And this isn't going to have any
impact on states in the past.

650
00:33:19,900 --> 00:33:26,360
So this is going to be
completely independent of St50--

651
00:33:26,360 --> 00:33:27,520
or sorry, S50.

652
00:33:27,520 --> 00:33:34,680
So this doesn't affect
S50 or S51 or S99.

653
00:33:34,680 --> 00:33:36,600
And this means that the--

654
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:38,160
yeah, because of
that independence,

655
00:33:38,160 --> 00:33:43,240
we can then drop the rewards.

656
00:33:43,240 --> 00:33:45,980
We can drop that from
the equation and--

657
00:33:45,980 --> 00:33:48,300
[INAUDIBLE]

658
00:33:48,300 --> 00:33:48,800
Yeah.

659
00:33:48,800 --> 00:33:50,666
And only look at--

660
00:33:50,666 --> 00:33:54,040
when looking at increasing the
likelihood of an action at time

661
00:33:54,040 --> 00:33:59,477
t, only use the future
rewards in that influence.

662
00:33:59,477 --> 00:34:01,560
It's a little bit hard to
write out mathematically

663
00:34:01,560 --> 00:34:03,960
why you can do that.

664
00:34:03,960 --> 00:34:06,360
I can maybe think
about whether there's

665
00:34:06,360 --> 00:34:09,199
a nice mathematical
expression for that,

666
00:34:09,199 --> 00:34:14,159
but it's really just about
causality in the world.

667
00:34:14,159 --> 00:34:16,230
And so basically, policy
behavior at time t

668
00:34:16,230 --> 00:34:20,350
is not going to influence
rewards at earlier time steps.

669
00:34:20,350 --> 00:34:21,270
Cool.

670
00:34:21,270 --> 00:34:22,989
I think that this equation--

671
00:34:22,989 --> 00:34:24,110
OK.

672
00:34:24,110 --> 00:34:26,409
I don't have the typo in
this equation, so, yeah.

673
00:34:26,409 --> 00:34:30,150
We're bringing this summation
inside the other one.

674
00:34:30,150 --> 00:34:34,350
And it's only going to be a
factor of the future time steps.

675
00:34:34,350 --> 00:34:37,190
And this means that for
this example right here,

676
00:34:37,190 --> 00:34:41,310
we will be decreasing
the likelihood of actions

677
00:34:41,310 --> 00:34:42,949
at the beginning
of the trajectory.

678
00:34:42,949 --> 00:34:47,070
And then the future summations
will become positive

679
00:34:47,070 --> 00:34:50,613
because you took a step forward.

680
00:34:50,613 --> 00:34:53,030
Then you'll actually start to
be increasing the likelihood

681
00:34:53,030 --> 00:34:54,519
of taking a small step forward.

682
00:34:54,519 --> 00:34:55,570
[INAUDIBLE]

683
00:34:55,570 --> 00:34:56,070
Yeah.

684
00:34:56,070 --> 00:34:57,487
So a very good
point is this still

685
00:34:57,487 --> 00:34:59,590
doesn't help if you
take a step forward

686
00:34:59,590 --> 00:35:01,350
and then you take
a step backwards.

687
00:35:01,350 --> 00:35:03,625
And the reason why it doesn't--

688
00:35:03,625 --> 00:35:06,250
the reason why that's actually
a little bit difficult to handle

689
00:35:06,250 --> 00:35:08,830
is that it's possible that
the step forwards actually

690
00:35:08,830 --> 00:35:11,190
cause you to fall
backwards, or cause

691
00:35:11,190 --> 00:35:14,374
you to take a step backwards.

692
00:35:14,374 --> 00:35:16,750
A big part of
reinforcement learning

693
00:35:16,750 --> 00:35:21,770
is thinking about what influence
does actions have on the future.

694
00:35:21,770 --> 00:35:23,710
And you need to learn
that from experience.

695
00:35:23,710 --> 00:35:28,392
And if you take a step forward,
it means that it could--

696
00:35:28,392 --> 00:35:29,850
from the algorithm's
point of view,

697
00:35:29,850 --> 00:35:32,910
actually, have caused something
bad to happen in the future,

698
00:35:32,910 --> 00:35:39,310
and so we can't
drop those rewards.

699
00:35:39,310 --> 00:35:42,190
So the question was,
if you say that you

700
00:35:42,190 --> 00:35:44,110
had a lot of really bad
stuff at the beginning

701
00:35:44,110 --> 00:35:46,650
and then you took a good action
later on in the trajectory,

702
00:35:46,650 --> 00:35:50,190
would you have enough
time to accumulate that?

703
00:35:50,190 --> 00:35:53,830
So in your particular
example, you started getting--

704
00:35:53,830 --> 00:35:57,110
capital T was equal to 10,
and you started seeing--

705
00:35:57,110 --> 00:35:59,790
you did something really
good at time step 6.

706
00:35:59,790 --> 00:36:02,670
Now, at time step 6, what
this will look like is

707
00:36:02,670 --> 00:36:10,510
you'll have grad
log a6 given S6.

708
00:36:10,510 --> 00:36:16,820
And then you'll have a summation
from t prime equals 6 to 10

709
00:36:16,820 --> 00:36:20,220
of our St prime, at prime.

710
00:36:20,220 --> 00:36:23,260
And so if you did get a high
reward from that action,

711
00:36:23,260 --> 00:36:25,900
immediately or in the
next four time steps,

712
00:36:25,900 --> 00:36:29,860
then you would actually increase
the likelihood on that action.

713
00:36:29,860 --> 00:36:33,820
Now, it's really going to depend
a lot on the reward function.

714
00:36:33,820 --> 00:36:37,155
So you're saying that for
fixed horizon problems,

715
00:36:37,155 --> 00:36:38,780
you're actually going
to be giving it--

716
00:36:38,780 --> 00:36:42,100
the gradient will be different
if you're summing over 10

717
00:36:42,100 --> 00:36:44,740
rewards versus over 4 rewards.

718
00:36:44,740 --> 00:36:46,460
So one of the things
that's important

719
00:36:46,460 --> 00:36:50,740
if you have a fixed
time horizon T is

720
00:36:50,740 --> 00:36:52,980
for this to be a proper state--

721
00:36:52,980 --> 00:36:55,340
and actually to be
truly Markovian-- you

722
00:36:55,340 --> 00:37:04,100
need it to contain information
about the future dynamics.

723
00:37:04,100 --> 00:37:08,620
And if it's fixed
at 10 and the policy

724
00:37:08,620 --> 00:37:11,320
doesn't know if the
horizon is about to finish,

725
00:37:11,320 --> 00:37:13,360
then it's actually not
a truly Markovian state.

726
00:37:13,360 --> 00:37:15,860
You need to actually give it
information about how many time

727
00:37:15,860 --> 00:37:17,660
steps it has left to
succeed, and then you

728
00:37:17,660 --> 00:37:19,182
can incorporate
that into the state.

729
00:37:19,182 --> 00:37:21,140
And so you would actually
have different states

730
00:37:21,140 --> 00:37:24,140
in those different
circumstances.

731
00:37:24,140 --> 00:37:25,280
OK.

732
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:28,227
So now another example for you.

733
00:37:28,227 --> 00:37:30,060
And this actually comes
back to the question

734
00:37:30,060 --> 00:37:32,700
that came up earlier,
although it's actually

735
00:37:32,700 --> 00:37:33,560
slightly different.

736
00:37:33,560 --> 00:37:36,100
So say that the robot--

737
00:37:36,100 --> 00:37:38,580
we now have a
version that is only

738
00:37:38,580 --> 00:37:40,340
depending on future rewards.

739
00:37:40,340 --> 00:37:42,360
We have the same
version of the problem,

740
00:37:42,360 --> 00:37:44,500
but now our policy is
a little bit better.

741
00:37:44,500 --> 00:37:46,200
One of the trajectories
falls forward.

742
00:37:46,200 --> 00:37:48,922
One of the trajectories
slowly stumbles forward.

743
00:37:48,922 --> 00:37:50,880
One of the trajectories
steadily walks forward.

744
00:37:50,880 --> 00:37:52,588
And one of the
trajectories runs forward.

745
00:37:52,588 --> 00:37:55,460

746
00:37:55,460 --> 00:37:58,180
My question for you is, what
will the gradient encourage

747
00:37:58,180 --> 00:38:01,780
the policy to do in this case?

748
00:38:01,780 --> 00:38:07,430
And we can use this version,
the updated version.

749
00:38:07,430 --> 00:38:09,970

750
00:38:09,970 --> 00:38:13,350
And so I'll encourage you to
pair up again and discuss this,

751
00:38:13,350 --> 00:38:15,555
and then we'll come
back in 2 minutes.

752
00:38:15,555 --> 00:38:18,950
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

753
00:38:18,950 --> 00:38:20,310

754
00:38:20,310 --> 00:38:20,810
OK.

755
00:38:20,810 --> 00:38:22,770
Let's come back.

756
00:38:22,770 --> 00:38:28,510
Please raise your hand if you
want to share your answer.

757
00:38:28,510 --> 00:38:29,010
OK?

758
00:38:29,010 --> 00:38:29,510
Yeah.

759
00:38:29,510 --> 00:38:31,850
So the first answer
was that it's

760
00:38:31,850 --> 00:38:34,545
going to get the highest reward
for the stuff that is moving

761
00:38:34,545 --> 00:38:35,670
forward at higher velocity.

762
00:38:35,670 --> 00:38:38,050
So it's going to encourage
it to do that, the highest

763
00:38:38,050 --> 00:38:39,155
velocity stuff the most.

764
00:38:39,155 --> 00:38:40,530
And you're saying
that even if it

765
00:38:40,530 --> 00:38:42,000
makes any sort of
forward progress,

766
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:44,890
it will still encourage
it to do that.

767
00:38:44,890 --> 00:38:46,858
So another hypothesis
was that if you go back

768
00:38:46,858 --> 00:38:48,650
to the previous example,
it might encourage

769
00:38:48,650 --> 00:38:50,930
it to fall forward and then
get back up again and then

770
00:38:50,930 --> 00:38:52,847
fall forward and get
back up again, especially

771
00:38:52,847 --> 00:38:55,155
if it's easy to fall forward.

772
00:38:55,155 --> 00:38:56,530
Now, getting up
again is actually

773
00:38:56,530 --> 00:38:58,572
a little bit more challenging,
so that might also

774
00:38:58,572 --> 00:39:00,410
be a little tricky.

775
00:39:00,410 --> 00:39:02,550
And so the thoughts there
was that the fourth one,

776
00:39:02,550 --> 00:39:04,040
if the reward is--

777
00:39:04,040 --> 00:39:06,290
it might dominate if that
one has a much higher reward

778
00:39:06,290 --> 00:39:07,810
than the others.

779
00:39:07,810 --> 00:39:11,490
If they're more even in terms
of velocity and it's just move

780
00:39:11,490 --> 00:39:13,730
forward, then it won't
necessarily dominate,

781
00:39:13,730 --> 00:39:15,970
and you'll do all four.

782
00:39:15,970 --> 00:39:20,697
So all of the rewards in these
cases will all be positive.

783
00:39:20,697 --> 00:39:22,530
I think what a lot of
people were getting at

784
00:39:22,530 --> 00:39:25,990
is that the reward of this
last one will be the highest,

785
00:39:25,990 --> 00:39:27,950
and so we'll be
encouraged to do this.

786
00:39:27,950 --> 00:39:30,730
But because all of
these 1, 2, and 3

787
00:39:30,730 --> 00:39:32,713
also have positive
rewards, the policy

788
00:39:32,713 --> 00:39:34,130
is actually also
still going to be

789
00:39:34,130 --> 00:39:36,250
encouraged to do those things.

790
00:39:36,250 --> 00:39:42,927
So basically, when this term is
nonnegative, when it's positive,

791
00:39:42,927 --> 00:39:45,010
you're still going to be
increasing the likelihood

792
00:39:45,010 --> 00:39:48,050
on the actions in
these trajectories.

793
00:39:48,050 --> 00:39:50,510
Now it won't do it as
frequently as running forward,

794
00:39:50,510 --> 00:39:53,730
but it will still be
encouraged to do that.

795
00:39:53,730 --> 00:39:55,610
And that might be a
little bit concerning

796
00:39:55,610 --> 00:39:58,168
because some of the
time, it will actually

797
00:39:58,168 --> 00:39:59,710
be encouraging it
to stumble forward.

798
00:39:59,710 --> 00:40:02,150
And ideally, we would
actually, in this case,

799
00:40:02,150 --> 00:40:06,130
want a policy that can
actually have the policy run

800
00:40:06,130 --> 00:40:08,400
as fast as possible.

801
00:40:08,400 --> 00:40:10,720
So the question was, is this
because of local minimum?

802
00:40:10,720 --> 00:40:13,480
It seems like with
this reward function,

803
00:40:13,480 --> 00:40:15,140
the best outcome
is to run forward,

804
00:40:15,140 --> 00:40:19,640
and so why are we getting
this kind of objective?

805
00:40:19,640 --> 00:40:23,140
The reason for this is
because the gradient is noisy.

806
00:40:23,140 --> 00:40:25,200
It comes down to variance.

807
00:40:25,200 --> 00:40:28,540
And if you do have a
policy that runs forward,

808
00:40:28,540 --> 00:40:31,360
it will encourage it to
keep on running forward.

809
00:40:31,360 --> 00:40:34,180
But when you have
other data in there,

810
00:40:34,180 --> 00:40:36,180
that other data will
influence it in some ways.

811
00:40:36,180 --> 00:40:38,613
And so it won't necessarily
converge to a local optimum,

812
00:40:38,613 --> 00:40:40,280
because at this point,
it will encourage

813
00:40:40,280 --> 00:40:41,650
it to run forward more.

814
00:40:41,650 --> 00:40:43,900
And then the next batch of
data will run forward more,

815
00:40:43,900 --> 00:40:45,900
and it'll encourage it
to run forward even more.

816
00:40:45,900 --> 00:40:48,960
So eventually, it will
converge to the global optimum,

817
00:40:48,960 --> 00:40:55,280
but it will get there more
slowly because the gradient has

818
00:40:55,280 --> 00:41:00,000
a lot of variance, depending
on the samples that you select.

819
00:41:00,000 --> 00:41:03,920
Will this encourage the
robot to roll forward?

820
00:41:03,920 --> 00:41:06,360
With this data, it
will not encourage

821
00:41:06,360 --> 00:41:09,880
it to roll forward unless some
weird combination of gradients

822
00:41:09,880 --> 00:41:11,440
causes that.

823
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:13,520
But yeah, generally,
it will not.

824
00:41:13,520 --> 00:41:15,320
If you had data of
rolling forward,

825
00:41:15,320 --> 00:41:17,665
then it would encourage
it to roll forward.

826
00:41:17,665 --> 00:41:19,040
So the question
is, can the agent

827
00:41:19,040 --> 00:41:20,748
learn from a subset
of characters instead

828
00:41:20,748 --> 00:41:22,640
of the whole trajectory?

829
00:41:22,640 --> 00:41:24,840
In this case, it will
learn from all future steps

830
00:41:24,840 --> 00:41:25,660
in the trajectory.

831
00:41:25,660 --> 00:41:28,600
So that is a subset,
but it might not be--

832
00:41:28,600 --> 00:41:30,220
and I guess-- in
the next lecture,

833
00:41:30,220 --> 00:41:35,080
we'll talk about more
sophisticated ways to estimate

834
00:41:35,080 --> 00:41:36,720
this--

835
00:41:36,720 --> 00:41:39,860
are smarter basically.

836
00:41:39,860 --> 00:41:40,360
Cool.

837
00:41:40,360 --> 00:41:42,193
So the thing that we
saw here is that things

838
00:41:42,193 --> 00:41:44,377
are sensitive to the scaling
of the reward function.

839
00:41:44,377 --> 00:41:46,460
And on the flip side, if
everything was negative--

840
00:41:46,460 --> 00:41:48,320
it came up in the
question before--

841
00:41:48,320 --> 00:41:50,880
we would actually only be
getting negative gradients

842
00:41:50,880 --> 00:41:53,640
to our policy and no
actual positive gradients.

843
00:41:53,640 --> 00:41:56,550
And that's actually
a big problem

844
00:41:56,550 --> 00:41:59,600
with this kind of approach
is that it depends

845
00:41:59,600 --> 00:42:01,853
on the scale of the reward.

846
00:42:01,853 --> 00:42:03,520
And there's actually
a very simple thing

847
00:42:03,520 --> 00:42:06,470
that we can do to fix this.

848
00:42:06,470 --> 00:42:09,550
And so in particular,
what we can do

849
00:42:09,550 --> 00:42:13,550
is when we are using
this gradient, which is

850
00:42:13,550 --> 00:42:20,350
the expectation of p of tau--

851
00:42:20,350 --> 00:42:24,710
I'll maybe do the first version
just to make it a bit shorter,

852
00:42:24,710 --> 00:42:32,350
but we get the gradient of
log p of tau times r of tau.

853
00:42:32,350 --> 00:42:35,790
And what we can do is instead
of having this be r of tau,

854
00:42:35,790 --> 00:42:40,230
we can actually
subtract a constant,

855
00:42:40,230 --> 00:42:45,980
where that constant could be
equal to the average reward.

856
00:42:45,980 --> 00:42:50,670

857
00:42:50,670 --> 00:42:53,562
And if we subtract the
average reward from this,

858
00:42:53,562 --> 00:42:58,510
this means that things that
are better than average

859
00:42:58,510 --> 00:43:00,190
will increase the
likelihood, and things

860
00:43:00,190 --> 00:43:03,550
that are below
average will actually

861
00:43:03,550 --> 00:43:05,390
lead us to decrease likelihood.

862
00:43:05,390 --> 00:43:08,030
So this will be negative.

863
00:43:08,030 --> 00:43:13,310
Now, you might be wondering,
can we just add this b here?

864
00:43:13,310 --> 00:43:16,150
Did I just make that up?

865
00:43:16,150 --> 00:43:17,070
Why can we do that?

866
00:43:17,070 --> 00:43:18,320
Why are we allowed to do that?

867
00:43:18,320 --> 00:43:23,470

868
00:43:23,470 --> 00:43:27,870
We can see what the effect
that this has on the gradient.

869
00:43:27,870 --> 00:43:30,790
And what we can show
is we can actually

870
00:43:30,790 --> 00:43:35,470
show that if you subtract
a constant in expectation,

871
00:43:35,470 --> 00:43:38,470
this has zero effect
on the gradient.

872
00:43:38,470 --> 00:43:45,950
And in particular-- so
this is the gradient of J.

873
00:43:45,950 --> 00:43:49,750
And if we look at what effect
this has on the gradient

874
00:43:49,750 --> 00:43:54,710
and expectation, we can look
at essentially the expectation

875
00:43:54,710 --> 00:44:03,960
of grad log p times b.

876
00:44:03,960 --> 00:44:07,540

877
00:44:07,540 --> 00:44:11,900
And it's possible to show
that this in expectation

878
00:44:11,900 --> 00:44:14,380
is actually equal to zero.

879
00:44:14,380 --> 00:44:23,100
And in particular, if we write
this out as the gradient of--

880
00:44:23,100 --> 00:44:26,180
or sorry, the expectation
is the integral

881
00:44:26,180 --> 00:44:39,460
of p of tau times grad
log p of tau times bd tau.

882
00:44:39,460 --> 00:44:43,340
We can then use the
same trick that we

883
00:44:43,340 --> 00:44:45,500
used before, but in reverse.

884
00:44:45,500 --> 00:44:59,140
This is equal to the integral
of grad p-theta of tau times bd

885
00:44:59,140 --> 00:45:01,700
tau.

886
00:45:01,700 --> 00:45:05,180
I'm a little bit out of
space, but the gradient

887
00:45:05,180 --> 00:45:09,404
can be moved to the
outside of the integral

888
00:45:09,404 --> 00:45:13,300
because this is a
linear operation.

889
00:45:13,300 --> 00:45:16,100
We can also move b
outside the integral

890
00:45:16,100 --> 00:45:17,940
because it's a constant.

891
00:45:17,940 --> 00:45:29,140
And so this is equal
to the gradient

892
00:45:29,140 --> 00:45:39,660
b times the gradient of the
integral of p-theta of tau d

893
00:45:39,660 --> 00:45:41,100
tau.

894
00:45:41,100 --> 00:45:45,860
Now this integral is just equal
to 1, and the gradient of 1

895
00:45:45,860 --> 00:45:47,540
is 0.

896
00:45:47,540 --> 00:45:57,180
So this is 1, this is
0, and b times 0 is 0.

897
00:45:57,180 --> 00:46:01,290
And so this means that
the expectation, this

898
00:46:01,290 --> 00:46:05,570
is going to have 0
effect on the gradient.

899
00:46:05,570 --> 00:46:11,490
And so just to maybe write that
out a little bit more cleanly,

900
00:46:11,490 --> 00:46:15,495
the expectation of the
gradient of log p times

901
00:46:15,495 --> 00:46:17,370
b-- so basically, the
fact that this baseline

902
00:46:17,370 --> 00:46:19,690
is having on this gradient.

903
00:46:19,690 --> 00:46:22,170
We can write that
as the integral.

904
00:46:22,170 --> 00:46:25,170
We can then use the
log trick in reverse.

905
00:46:25,170 --> 00:46:27,900
We can move b and the gradient
outside of the integral.

906
00:46:27,900 --> 00:46:30,450

907
00:46:30,450 --> 00:46:33,890
This integral is equal to
1, the gradient of 1 is 0,

908
00:46:33,890 --> 00:46:35,650
and then we get 0.

909
00:46:35,650 --> 00:46:38,410
So this means that we
can add this baseline.

910
00:46:38,410 --> 00:46:40,650
So the reason why this
constant term-- the question

911
00:46:40,650 --> 00:46:43,930
was this constant term wouldn't
be helpful if we could actually

912
00:46:43,930 --> 00:46:45,868
truly evaluate the expectation.

913
00:46:45,868 --> 00:46:46,910
And yeah, that's correct.

914
00:46:46,910 --> 00:46:50,410
So if we had this expectation
and could measure this exactly,

915
00:46:50,410 --> 00:46:51,577
then we wouldn't need this.

916
00:46:51,577 --> 00:46:53,410
The reason why this is
helpful is because we

917
00:46:53,410 --> 00:46:56,610
have this expectation.

918
00:46:56,610 --> 00:46:58,910
Basically, you can think
of it as reducing--

919
00:46:58,910 --> 00:47:01,490
it's a way to reduce the
variance of your estimate

920
00:47:01,490 --> 00:47:06,090
of this expectation, and it's
an unbiased way to do that.

921
00:47:06,090 --> 00:47:11,970
So in particular, as long as
the baseline is a constant,

922
00:47:11,970 --> 00:47:13,450
then it won't
change the behavior

923
00:47:13,450 --> 00:47:14,742
of the gradient in expectation.

924
00:47:14,742 --> 00:47:16,270
This means that it's unbiased.

925
00:47:16,270 --> 00:47:20,170
This is a term from statistics,
talking about effect

926
00:47:20,170 --> 00:47:21,470
on random variables.

927
00:47:21,470 --> 00:47:24,690
And you can show that it reduces
the variance of your estimate

928
00:47:24,690 --> 00:47:26,220
of the gradient.

929
00:47:26,220 --> 00:47:27,970
So the question is,
is b really a constant

930
00:47:27,970 --> 00:47:30,890
and seems to depend
on the trajectories?

931
00:47:30,890 --> 00:47:36,970
We'll use the same value
of b for an entire batch,

932
00:47:36,970 --> 00:47:38,830
for all of the examples.

933
00:47:38,830 --> 00:47:43,010
And so we'll collect a batch of
data, then we will compute b,

934
00:47:43,010 --> 00:47:47,770
and we'll then use b
for all of the updates

935
00:47:47,770 --> 00:47:49,810
on that batch of data.

936
00:47:49,810 --> 00:47:53,210
So how does it reduce the
variance of the gradient?

937
00:47:53,210 --> 00:47:54,710
You can write down--

938
00:47:54,710 --> 00:47:56,820
so this is the expectation
of the gradient.

939
00:47:56,820 --> 00:47:58,680
You can write down the
variance analytically

940
00:47:58,680 --> 00:48:03,546
and show that it
reduces the variance.

941
00:48:03,546 --> 00:48:05,520
I don't want to go
through the math

942
00:48:05,520 --> 00:48:07,560
because we have some other
stuff to get through.

943
00:48:07,560 --> 00:48:09,320
But intuitively, you
can think about it

944
00:48:09,320 --> 00:48:14,000
in terms of this example of--

945
00:48:14,000 --> 00:48:17,890
it will encourage it to do
more of the above-average stuff

946
00:48:17,890 --> 00:48:19,390
and less of the
below-average stuff.

947
00:48:19,390 --> 00:48:21,906

948
00:48:21,906 --> 00:48:24,280
I could also mention that
average reward is actually

949
00:48:24,280 --> 00:48:26,000
not the optimal baseline.

950
00:48:26,000 --> 00:48:29,520
You can actually
derive the variance,

951
00:48:29,520 --> 00:48:34,360
and then take the derivative
of the variance side equal to 0

952
00:48:34,360 --> 00:48:36,840
to find the optimal baseline.

953
00:48:36,840 --> 00:48:38,600
But average reward
is pretty good.

954
00:48:38,600 --> 00:48:41,380
The optimal baseline
is something--

955
00:48:41,380 --> 00:48:42,860
it's very close
to average reward,

956
00:48:42,860 --> 00:48:44,693
but there's some
additional weighting terms.

957
00:48:44,693 --> 00:48:47,160

958
00:48:47,160 --> 00:48:48,120
Cool.

959
00:48:48,120 --> 00:48:52,360
So we have one more episode
of what does the gradient do.

960
00:48:52,360 --> 00:48:55,280
And we're going to look
at a different example.

961
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:59,040
In this case, the robot
is folding a jacket.

962
00:48:59,040 --> 00:49:01,880
And the reward is 1
if it's neatly folded,

963
00:49:01,880 --> 00:49:04,300
0.5 if it's folded
with some wrinkles,

964
00:49:04,300 --> 00:49:07,200
and 0 if it's not folded.

965
00:49:07,200 --> 00:49:09,600
We're going to again
sample from our policy.

966
00:49:09,600 --> 00:49:12,020
We have some samples of
not touching the jacket,

967
00:49:12,020 --> 00:49:15,080
folding only the sleeves but
not folding the full thing,

968
00:49:15,080 --> 00:49:17,140
flattening the jacket
but not folding it,

969
00:49:17,140 --> 00:49:20,440
and one trajectory where
it does fold the jacket.

970
00:49:20,440 --> 00:49:24,480
And my question is, with the
baseline in here this time,

971
00:49:24,480 --> 00:49:26,880
what does the
gradient look like?

972
00:49:26,880 --> 00:49:32,960
The rewards for 1, 2, and 3
will be 0, and the reward for 4

973
00:49:32,960 --> 00:49:34,340
will be positive.

974
00:49:34,340 --> 00:49:36,300
And so this means that
because of our baseline,

975
00:49:36,300 --> 00:49:38,320
the gradient for
4 will be positive

976
00:49:38,320 --> 00:49:39,900
and will encourage
this behavior.

977
00:49:39,900 --> 00:49:43,040
And then we'll discourage all
the behavior for 1, 2, and 3.

978
00:49:43,040 --> 00:49:45,040
Now, in some ways,
this is good because it

979
00:49:45,040 --> 00:49:47,982
means that we are encouraging
the good behavior.

980
00:49:47,982 --> 00:49:49,440
But there are also
some things that

981
00:49:49,440 --> 00:49:51,920
are a little bit
dissatisfying about this.

982
00:49:51,920 --> 00:49:55,630
The first is that the
gradient for 1, 2, and 3

983
00:49:55,630 --> 00:49:57,830
is going to be identical
for all of them.

984
00:49:57,830 --> 00:50:01,190
There isn't any
differentiation between them.

985
00:50:01,190 --> 00:50:03,690
And some of these are
actually better than others.

986
00:50:03,690 --> 00:50:07,150
And this is, in some ways, a
result of the reward function

987
00:50:07,150 --> 00:50:11,040
that we're using, which is
more sparse than it could be.

988
00:50:11,040 --> 00:50:12,790
And the other thing
is that we'll actually

989
00:50:12,790 --> 00:50:17,110
see some techniques in Friday's
lecture that would actually

990
00:50:17,110 --> 00:50:18,990
be able to leverage
some of the good parts

991
00:50:18,990 --> 00:50:20,110
of these trajectories,
because there

992
00:50:20,110 --> 00:50:21,860
is a good thing about
folding the sleeves,

993
00:50:21,860 --> 00:50:24,030
and it would be nice to
be able to use that data

994
00:50:24,030 --> 00:50:25,610
to encourage the policy.

995
00:50:25,610 --> 00:50:27,950
And right now, it will
actually be discouraged

996
00:50:27,950 --> 00:50:30,990
and have negative gradients
for this trajectory.

997
00:50:30,990 --> 00:50:32,670
So in some ways, this is good.

998
00:50:32,670 --> 00:50:34,790
So it will encourage
folding, but the gradient

999
00:50:34,790 --> 00:50:38,230
is constant for
trajectories 1, 2, and 3.

1000
00:50:38,230 --> 00:50:44,470
And on Friday, we'll see ways to
make this a little bit better.

1001
00:50:44,470 --> 00:50:46,910
And one of the takeaways
here is that policy gradient

1002
00:50:46,910 --> 00:50:49,910
is still noisy and is
still high variance.

1003
00:50:49,910 --> 00:50:51,390
And it really is
going to work best

1004
00:50:51,390 --> 00:50:54,960
if you have dense rewards rather
than rewards that are sparse.

1005
00:50:54,960 --> 00:50:57,210
And it'll work best if you
have large batches of data,

1006
00:50:57,210 --> 00:50:59,750
because if you didn't actually
sample this fourth trajectory

1007
00:50:59,750 --> 00:51:05,990
and you only sampled failures,
then you would have 0 gradient.

1008
00:51:05,990 --> 00:51:09,110
And you wouldn't actually
be able to improve.

1009
00:51:09,110 --> 00:51:13,250
So the question was, can we just
improve the reward function?

1010
00:51:13,250 --> 00:51:13,750
Yes.

1011
00:51:13,750 --> 00:51:17,270
So if we had a more
dense reward function,

1012
00:51:17,270 --> 00:51:20,030
that would definitely
help in these cases.

1013
00:51:20,030 --> 00:51:26,430
There are actually scenarios
still where we could do better.

1014
00:51:26,430 --> 00:51:28,770
Especially if you, for
example, folded the sleeves,

1015
00:51:28,770 --> 00:51:30,350
you got positive
reward for this,

1016
00:51:30,350 --> 00:51:32,470
and then dropped it on
the ground or something

1017
00:51:32,470 --> 00:51:34,510
after that, then that's
still dissatisfying

1018
00:51:34,510 --> 00:51:36,910
because we wouldn't use the
first part successfully.

1019
00:51:36,910 --> 00:51:39,930
And we'll actually
see how to get

1020
00:51:39,930 --> 00:51:42,285
a good gradient for that first
part on Friday, but yeah.

1021
00:51:42,285 --> 00:51:43,910
If you had a more
dense reward function

1022
00:51:43,910 --> 00:51:45,610
for this particular
set of trajectories,

1023
00:51:45,610 --> 00:51:48,122
that would work a lot better.

1024
00:51:48,122 --> 00:51:50,330
Oh, so the question here
is, when we do the baseline,

1025
00:51:50,330 --> 00:51:54,020
are we also using this trick
to only use future rewards?

1026
00:51:54,020 --> 00:51:55,900
You can combine them.

1027
00:51:55,900 --> 00:51:57,982
You need to be a little bit--

1028
00:51:57,982 --> 00:51:59,940
you need to think a little
bit potentially more

1029
00:51:59,940 --> 00:52:02,660
carefully about what
the baseline will be,

1030
00:52:02,660 --> 00:52:06,115
because some of the rewards--

1031
00:52:06,115 --> 00:52:08,240
you'll be summing over
fewer things or more things,

1032
00:52:08,240 --> 00:52:10,180
depending on where you
are in the trajectory,

1033
00:52:10,180 --> 00:52:12,577
but you can definitely
combine them.

1034
00:52:12,577 --> 00:52:14,660
So the question was, if
we're using average reward

1035
00:52:14,660 --> 00:52:17,440
as the baseline, do we need
to keep updating the term?

1036
00:52:17,440 --> 00:52:20,030
Do we need to use
data from the policy?

1037
00:52:20,030 --> 00:52:21,780
So yes, you'll want
to keep on updating it

1038
00:52:21,780 --> 00:52:24,980
because as the policy
gets better, above average

1039
00:52:24,980 --> 00:52:26,660
and below average will change.

1040
00:52:26,660 --> 00:52:30,450
And you'll want to recompute it
on your latest batch of data.

1041
00:52:30,450 --> 00:52:33,860

1042
00:52:33,860 --> 00:52:35,340
Cool.

1043
00:52:35,340 --> 00:52:37,680
So one more thing on this
version of the algorithm,

1044
00:52:37,680 --> 00:52:40,882
and then we'll move on
to one last version,

1045
00:52:40,882 --> 00:52:42,340
in terms of how to
implement it, we

1046
00:52:42,340 --> 00:52:47,060
talked about-- we derived
the gradient like this.

1047
00:52:47,060 --> 00:52:50,900
And to actually implement
this in practice,

1048
00:52:50,900 --> 00:52:53,100
you could, in
principle, actually

1049
00:52:53,100 --> 00:52:56,740
just run a backward pass for
all of these different terms.

1050
00:52:56,740 --> 00:52:58,720
But if you computed all
of these individually,

1051
00:52:58,720 --> 00:53:01,740
then you'd have to do n
times t backward passes,

1052
00:53:01,740 --> 00:53:04,940
and this would be
rather inefficient.

1053
00:53:04,940 --> 00:53:08,100
And it'd be nice if we
could just actually do

1054
00:53:08,100 --> 00:53:10,300
automatic differentiation
on the full objective

1055
00:53:10,300 --> 00:53:14,260
and only do one forward
pass and one backward pass.

1056
00:53:14,260 --> 00:53:15,880
And we can do that.

1057
00:53:15,880 --> 00:53:17,900
So it's possible to
implement essentially

1058
00:53:17,900 --> 00:53:20,100
a surrogate objective
whose gradient

1059
00:53:20,100 --> 00:53:22,952
is the same as this objective.

1060
00:53:22,952 --> 00:53:24,660
I'm calling it surrogate
because it's not

1061
00:53:24,660 --> 00:53:27,820
the same as the very first
objective of maximizing expected

1062
00:53:27,820 --> 00:53:30,027
rewards.

1063
00:53:30,027 --> 00:53:31,860
But you can write down
a surrogate objective

1064
00:53:31,860 --> 00:53:33,540
for this particular example.

1065
00:53:33,540 --> 00:53:35,740
This is what the surrogate
objective looks like.

1066
00:53:35,740 --> 00:53:40,660
Note that I actually do have a
typo on these equations, where

1067
00:53:40,660 --> 00:53:42,840
this second sum should
be inside the first sum.

1068
00:53:42,840 --> 00:53:43,960
That's maybe a small note.

1069
00:53:43,960 --> 00:53:46,140
I'll see if I can fix that.

1070
00:53:46,140 --> 00:53:48,750
But, yeah, you can implement
a surrogate objective,

1071
00:53:48,750 --> 00:53:52,130
and then use backprop
and autodiff software

1072
00:53:52,130 --> 00:53:54,430
to just do one backward pass.

1073
00:53:54,430 --> 00:53:58,255

1074
00:53:58,255 --> 00:54:00,130
And what this actually
looks like in practice

1075
00:54:00,130 --> 00:54:02,338
is this would actually look
like a cross-entropy loss

1076
00:54:02,338 --> 00:54:04,930
for a discrete action policy,
or something like squared error

1077
00:54:04,930 --> 00:54:06,730
for our Gaussian policy.

1078
00:54:06,730 --> 00:54:09,250
You could also use more
sophisticated policies,

1079
00:54:09,250 --> 00:54:12,990
like diffusion or something
like that as well.

1080
00:54:12,990 --> 00:54:13,490
OK.

1081
00:54:13,490 --> 00:54:17,970
So summary so far, we
showed how to estimate

1082
00:54:17,970 --> 00:54:21,810
the gradient of the RL objective
using this log gradient trick.

1083
00:54:21,810 --> 00:54:24,810
This corresponds to weighing
the policy likelihood

1084
00:54:24,810 --> 00:54:26,410
by future rewards.

1085
00:54:26,410 --> 00:54:30,330
And we found that subtracting a
baseline, like average reward,

1086
00:54:30,330 --> 00:54:34,290
will also further
improve the gradient.

1087
00:54:34,290 --> 00:54:37,490
Even with these two things,
the gradient is still noisy,

1088
00:54:37,490 --> 00:54:40,730
and so it's best with large
batches and dense rewards.

1089
00:54:40,730 --> 00:54:42,730
This is also our first
online reinforcement

1090
00:54:42,730 --> 00:54:44,230
learning algorithm,
where we iterate

1091
00:54:44,230 --> 00:54:47,490
between collecting a batch
of data with our policy

1092
00:54:47,490 --> 00:54:49,030
and improve our policy.

1093
00:54:49,030 --> 00:54:53,130
And it formalizes this
trial-and-error learning

1094
00:54:53,130 --> 00:54:55,047
process.

1095
00:54:55,047 --> 00:54:57,130
And the intuition for
everything we've done so far

1096
00:54:57,130 --> 00:54:58,850
is do more of the
high-reward stuff

1097
00:54:58,850 --> 00:55:01,810
and less of the
low-reward stuff.

1098
00:55:01,810 --> 00:55:02,310
OK.

1099
00:55:02,310 --> 00:55:04,450
Now, the last thing
we'll talk about today

1100
00:55:04,450 --> 00:55:08,490
is what else is troublesome
about policy gradients?

1101
00:55:08,490 --> 00:55:11,730
And one thing that we haven't
really talked about that much

1102
00:55:11,730 --> 00:55:14,250
is the details of
the sampling process.

1103
00:55:14,250 --> 00:55:18,050
And this is what our gradient
and our algorithm looks like.

1104
00:55:18,050 --> 00:55:22,450
And note that in
this gradient, this

1105
00:55:22,450 --> 00:55:25,410
assumes that we're
sampling from pi-theta.

1106
00:55:25,410 --> 00:55:30,650
And we change theta in step 3.

1107
00:55:30,650 --> 00:55:34,970
And this means that every single
time we take a single gradient

1108
00:55:34,970 --> 00:55:41,060
step, we need to recollect
data to estimate our gradient.

1109
00:55:41,060 --> 00:55:42,810
And this is kind of
sad because usually we

1110
00:55:42,810 --> 00:55:45,210
do thousands and thousands
of gradient steps

1111
00:55:45,210 --> 00:55:46,030
on neural networks.

1112
00:55:46,030 --> 00:55:49,320

1113
00:55:49,320 --> 00:55:53,080
So yeah, that seems
like a problem.

1114
00:55:53,080 --> 00:55:55,660
And what this is called
is it's called on-policy.

1115
00:55:55,660 --> 00:55:59,040
It means that the gradient
that we are measuring

1116
00:55:59,040 --> 00:56:03,120
requires data using
our exact policy.

1117
00:56:03,120 --> 00:56:04,800
And we can think about--

1118
00:56:04,800 --> 00:56:07,480
this is an attribute of an
online RL algorithm, where

1119
00:56:07,480 --> 00:56:10,960
on-policy only uses data
from the current policy,

1120
00:56:10,960 --> 00:56:13,000
and off-policy means
that you can reuse data

1121
00:56:13,000 --> 00:56:14,970
from other past policies.

1122
00:56:14,970 --> 00:56:17,840

1123
00:56:17,840 --> 00:56:22,220
So can we derive an off-policy
version of the policy gradient?

1124
00:56:22,220 --> 00:56:24,480
And it's only going to
be a little off-policy.

1125
00:56:24,480 --> 00:56:25,600
And by that, I mean,
we're just going

1126
00:56:25,600 --> 00:56:27,600
to try to be able to take
more than one gradient

1127
00:56:27,600 --> 00:56:31,520
step on a single batch of data.

1128
00:56:31,520 --> 00:56:34,782
And to do this, we can
use importance sampling.

1129
00:56:34,782 --> 00:56:37,240
And importance sampling is
actually a really cool technique

1130
00:56:37,240 --> 00:56:41,640
that's useful outside of
reinforcement learning as well.

1131
00:56:41,640 --> 00:56:45,520
And it's thinking
about this scenario

1132
00:56:45,520 --> 00:56:50,560
where you want to estimate
a quantity, an expectation,

1133
00:56:50,560 --> 00:56:52,900
without actually sampling
from that distribution.

1134
00:56:52,900 --> 00:57:00,760
So say you have some expectation
using samples from p of x,

1135
00:57:00,760 --> 00:57:04,560
and you want to
estimate some function

1136
00:57:04,560 --> 00:57:07,440
f of x using those
samples, but say

1137
00:57:07,440 --> 00:57:13,000
that you only have access to the
ability to sample from q of x.

1138
00:57:13,000 --> 00:57:15,800
So this is usually called your
proposal distribution that's

1139
00:57:15,800 --> 00:57:19,960
able to propose samples to use.

1140
00:57:19,960 --> 00:57:21,260
This is your old policy.

1141
00:57:21,260 --> 00:57:23,100
We have samples
from our old policy,

1142
00:57:23,100 --> 00:57:26,640
but we want to be able to
estimate a function of samples

1143
00:57:26,640 --> 00:57:30,840
of our new policy after
we've taken a gradient step.

1144
00:57:30,840 --> 00:57:34,080
So what you can do in
this case if we write down

1145
00:57:34,080 --> 00:57:40,640
this expectation as an integral,
it looks something like this.

1146
00:57:40,640 --> 00:57:46,950
And as long as q of x
has reasonable support,

1147
00:57:46,950 --> 00:57:48,870
we can write this as--

1148
00:57:48,870 --> 00:57:57,700
we can add in this term that
equals 1 into our equation.

1149
00:57:57,700 --> 00:58:00,350

1150
00:58:00,350 --> 00:58:03,110
And then we can now actually
write this as an expectation

1151
00:58:03,110 --> 00:58:05,350
with respect to q.

1152
00:58:05,350 --> 00:58:13,030
So this is equal to an
expectation with respect

1153
00:58:13,030 --> 00:58:24,950
to q of p of x divided
by q of x times f of x.

1154
00:58:24,950 --> 00:58:27,710
And so this means that we
can actually estimate--

1155
00:58:27,710 --> 00:58:33,310
we can get this expectation
by sampling from q

1156
00:58:33,310 --> 00:58:36,770
and then weighting
the quantity by p/q.

1157
00:58:36,770 --> 00:58:38,950
[INAUDIBLE]

1158
00:58:38,950 --> 00:58:40,150
So that's pretty cool.

1159
00:58:40,150 --> 00:58:42,830
This means that we can
use principal samples

1160
00:58:42,830 --> 00:58:47,710
from our old policy
q, weight them

1161
00:58:47,710 --> 00:58:50,870
by the probability of the sample
under our new policy divided

1162
00:58:50,870 --> 00:58:57,070
by the probability under the
old policy times the quantity.

1163
00:58:57,070 --> 00:59:00,230
So we showed for
this simple case

1164
00:59:00,230 --> 00:59:04,870
of p of x and q of x in terms
of actually applying this

1165
00:59:04,870 --> 00:59:08,670
to our policy.

1166
00:59:08,670 --> 00:59:12,930
We can take our expected rewards
with regard to p of theta

1167
00:59:12,930 --> 00:59:15,190
if we instead have some
previous policy that we've

1168
00:59:15,190 --> 00:59:16,550
collected samples from.

1169
00:59:16,550 --> 00:59:18,950
We can then measure
our objective

1170
00:59:18,950 --> 00:59:21,670
in terms of the samples
from that policy, where

1171
00:59:21,670 --> 00:59:26,150
we weight the reward by the new
policy divided by the old policy

1172
00:59:26,150 --> 00:59:27,950
probability.

1173
00:59:27,950 --> 00:59:32,510
Now, note that this requires
your proposal distribution

1174
00:59:32,510 --> 00:59:38,412
to have nonzero
support in scenarios

1175
00:59:38,412 --> 00:59:41,460
where your new policy
has probability.

1176
00:59:41,460 --> 00:59:44,060
This is actually very important
for it to work if q of x is 0.

1177
00:59:44,060 --> 00:59:47,580
This is not poorly defined.

1178
00:59:47,580 --> 00:59:49,700
But that's really
the only catch here.

1179
00:59:49,700 --> 00:59:51,820
And in general, this
importance sampling technique

1180
00:59:51,820 --> 00:59:54,345
is really going to work
best when p and q--

1181
00:59:54,345 --> 00:59:56,220
or when your old policy
and your new policy--

1182
00:59:56,220 --> 00:59:57,680
are very similar to each other.

1183
00:59:57,680 --> 01:00:00,980

1184
01:00:00,980 --> 01:00:02,480
Now there's some details--

1185
01:00:02,480 --> 01:00:04,160
we've shown this in
terms of p of tau,

1186
01:00:04,160 --> 01:00:07,980
now how do we actually do this
in terms of pi of a given S?

1187
01:00:07,980 --> 01:00:10,780
That actually gets a
little bit trickier.

1188
01:00:10,780 --> 01:00:15,780
We can write down p
of tau and this ratio

1189
01:00:15,780 --> 01:00:19,580
as the product of these
different probabilities.

1190
01:00:19,580 --> 01:00:23,180
Unfortunately, some of the
probabilities cancel out.

1191
01:00:23,180 --> 01:00:29,552
But when we then try to
put these probabilities

1192
01:00:29,552 --> 01:00:32,220
into our update, we're
going to get something

1193
01:00:32,220 --> 01:00:37,140
like this, where we
then have this product

1194
01:00:37,140 --> 01:00:41,140
of these probability ratios.

1195
01:00:41,140 --> 01:00:43,420
And now, unfortunately,
this product

1196
01:00:43,420 --> 01:00:46,460
can actually become
very small or very large

1197
01:00:46,460 --> 01:00:49,820
if the length of your
trajectory is very large,

1198
01:00:49,820 --> 01:00:51,940
because you're going
to be multiplying

1199
01:00:51,940 --> 01:00:53,460
many, many numbers together.

1200
01:00:53,460 --> 01:00:55,860
If you have 100 numbers that
you're multiplying together,

1201
01:00:55,860 --> 01:00:58,800
if they're above 1, that
might start to get very large.

1202
01:00:58,800 --> 01:01:02,140
If they're below 1, that
might get to 0 very quickly.

1203
01:01:02,140 --> 01:01:06,100
And so in practice, we often
will consider the expectations

1204
01:01:06,100 --> 01:01:09,260
over time steps instead
of trajectories.

1205
01:01:09,260 --> 01:01:12,300
And that also has a little
bit of a hitch, which

1206
01:01:12,300 --> 01:01:16,500
is that this needs to be
over state action pairs

1207
01:01:16,500 --> 01:01:19,660
rather than actions
given states.

1208
01:01:19,660 --> 01:01:22,420
But often in practice,
we can approximate

1209
01:01:22,420 --> 01:01:24,440
this ratio as equal to 1.

1210
01:01:24,440 --> 01:01:27,720
This is incorrect, but it's
the best that we can do.

1211
01:01:27,720 --> 01:01:29,420
And then we can
get some final form

1212
01:01:29,420 --> 01:01:34,370
where we get this ratio of
new policy over old policy.

1213
01:01:34,370 --> 01:01:37,970

1214
01:01:37,970 --> 01:01:40,330
Cool.

1215
01:01:40,330 --> 01:01:41,903
The full algorithm
looks the same,

1216
01:01:41,903 --> 01:01:43,570
except now we can
take multiple gradient

1217
01:01:43,570 --> 01:01:45,410
steps on the same batch.

1218
01:01:45,410 --> 01:01:46,110
This is good.

1219
01:01:46,110 --> 01:01:50,320
And then we have this new
form for our gradient.

1220
01:01:50,320 --> 01:01:53,130

1221
01:01:53,130 --> 01:01:54,600
OK.

1222
01:01:54,600 --> 01:01:57,710
We'll talk a little
bit about some tricks.

1223
01:01:57,710 --> 01:02:01,170
We don't have time to
cover KL constraints today,

1224
01:02:01,170 --> 01:02:03,930
but to review things, we
talked about online RL.

1225
01:02:03,930 --> 01:02:05,590
It's an on-policy algorithm.

1226
01:02:05,590 --> 01:02:07,830
We talked about baselines
for reducing variance.

1227
01:02:07,830 --> 01:02:10,550
And we also just derived this
off-policy policy gradient,

1228
01:02:10,550 --> 01:02:13,090
which uses importance
sampling and allows

1229
01:02:13,090 --> 01:02:14,720
us to apply multiple
gradient steps.

1230
01:02:14,720 --> 01:02:16,970
The intuition is to do more
high-reward stuff and less

1231
01:02:16,970 --> 01:02:18,110
low-reward stuff.

1232
01:02:18,110 --> 01:02:20,250
And the gradient is
still very noisy and best

1233
01:02:20,250 --> 01:02:24,610
with large batches
and dense rewards.

1234
01:02:24,610 --> 01:02:25,350
Cool.

1235
01:02:25,350 --> 01:02:26,350
And that's it for today.

1236
01:02:26,350 --> 01:02:28,642
Next time, we'll talk about
actor-critic methods, which

1237
01:02:28,642 --> 01:02:30,910
we'll build closely on
what we talked about today,

1238
01:02:30,910 --> 01:02:32,840
including PPO.

1239
01:02:32,840 --> 01:02:37,000

